ENTRY 9583 {"id":9583,"kind":"pi.tool-result","messages":[{"role":"toolResult","text":"grep: packages/vendor/agent-sandbox-client/test: No such file or directory\npackages/vendor/agent-sandbox-client/src/commands.ts:57: * before an exit event was returned.\npackages/vendor/agent-sandbox-client/src/commands.ts:147:  private exitSeen = false;\npackages/vendor/agent-sandbox-client/src/commands.ts:357:    if (this.streamEnded || (this.terminal && !this.exitSeen)) {\npackages/vendor/agent-sandbox-client/src/commands.ts:369:        this.exitSeen = true;\npackages/vendor/agent-sandbox-client/src/commands.ts:375:      if (this.exitSeen) {\npackages/vendor/agent-sandbox-client/src/commands.ts:376:        // A failure while draining the stream's tail, after a clean exit,\npackages/vendor/agent-sandbox-client/src/process.ts:252: * (done) only after an \"exit\" event was yielded; a stream that ends any\n   590\t        { cause: err },\n   591\t      );\n   592\t    }\n   593\t\n   594\t    const status = (sandboxObj.status as Record<string, unknown>) ?? {};\n   595\t    const podIPs = Array.isArray(status.podIPs) ? status.podIPs : [];\n   596\t    return selectPodIP(podIPs) || undefined;\n   597\t  }\n   598\t\n   599\t  /**\n   600\t   * Marks the handle closed and ends its tracing lifecycle span (if any).\n   601\t   * Does NOT delete the SandboxClaim from Kubernetes.\n   602\t   * Use this to release local resources without destroying the live claim —\n   603\t   * e.g. SandboxClient.getSandbox() evicting a stale cached handle whose claim\n   604\t   * may no longer be owned by it.\n   605\t   */\n   606\t  async closeLocal(): Promise<void> {\n   607\t    this._isClosed = true;\n   608\t    this.connectAbortController.abort(\n   609\t      new SandboxClosedError(\"Sandbox handle is closing\"),\n   610\t    );\n   611\t    await this.releaseLocal();\n   612\t  }\n   613\t\n   614\t  /**\n   615\t   * Closes the handle and deletes the SandboxClaim.\n   616\t   *\n   617\t   * A missing claim (404) is treated as success. Any other failure — including\n   618\t   * the cleanup timeout — is re-thrown as a {@link SandboxError} so callers can\n   619\t   * retry; the handle is still marked closed and the claim may be re-deleted\n   620\t   * via {@link SandboxClient.deleteSandbox}.\n   621\t   */\n   622\t  async close(): Promise<void> {\n   623\t    // Prevent new work immediately so the in-flight count stabilises.\n   624\t    this._isClosed = true;\n   625\t    this.connectAbortController.abort(\n   626\t      new SandboxClosedError(\"Sandbox handle is closing\"),\n   627\t    );\n   628\t\n   629\t    // Drain connected in-flight work; give up after CLEANUP_TIMEOUT_MS, or as\n   630\t    // soon as a concurrent closeLocal() fires the lifecycle abort, so close()\n    59\texport async function runBridge(options: BridgeOptions): Promise<number> {\n    60\t  const { stdin, stdout, stderr } = options;\n    61\t  let proc: RemoteProcess;\n    62\t  try {\n    63\t    proc = await options.start(options.command, options.args, {\n    64\t      onStdout: (chunk) => void stdout.write(chunk),\n    65\t      onStderr: (chunk) => void stderr.write(chunk),\n    66\t    });\n    67\t  } catch (error) {\n    68\t    stderr.write(`bridge: cannot start ${options.command}: ${describe(error)}\\n`);\n    69\t    return 1;\n    70\t  }\n    71\t  // Tells the parent that the cold start (the CPU-heavy part) is over; see StartGate.\n    72\t  stderr.write(`${BRIDGE_READY}\\n`);\n    73\t\n    74\t  const exited = proc.wait().then(\n    75\t    (result): Outcome => ({ kind: \"exit\", exitCode: toExitCode(result.exitCode) }),\n    76\t    (error: unknown): Outcome => ({\n    77\t      kind: \"fatal\",\n    78\t      reason: `remote stream lost: ${describe(error)}`,\n    79\t    }),\n    80\t  );\n    81\t  const outputFailed = new Promise<Outcome>((resolve) =>\n    82\t    stdout.once(\"error\", (error) =>\n    83\t      resolve({ kind: \"fatal\", reason: `stdout write failed: ${describe(error)}` }),\n    84\t    ),\n    85\t  );\n    86\t  const pumped = (async (): Promise<Outcome> => {\n    87\t    try {\n    88\t      for await (const chunk of stdin) {\n    89\t        await proc.write(chunk as Uint8Array);\n    90\t      }\n    91\t      await proc.closeStdin();\n    92\t      return { kind: \"stdin-done\" };\n    93\t    } catch (error) {\n    94\t      return { kind: \"fatal\", reason: `stdin write failed: ${describe(error)}` };\n    95\t    }\n    96\t  })();\n    97\t\n    98\t  let outcome = await Promise.race([exited, pumped, outputFailed]);\n    99\t  if (outcome.kind === \"stdin-done\") {\n   100\t    // Stdin ended cleanly: pi-env exits on EOF, so wait for it.\n   101\t    outcome = await Promise.race([exited, outputFailed]);\n   102\t  }\n   103\t  try {\n   104\t    if (outcome.kind === \"fatal\") {\n   105\t      stderr.write(`bridge: ${outcome.reason}\\n`);\n   106\t      return 1;\n   107\t    }\n   108\t    if (outcome.kind === \"exit\") await flush(stdout);\n   109\t    return outcome.kind === \"exit\" ? outcome.exitCode : 1;\n   110\t  } finally {\n   111\t    await proc.close().catch(() => undefined);\n   112\t  }\n    18\t  /** Resolves on this caller's turn. The returned release hands the turn on; calling it again is a no-op. */\n    19\t  async acquire(): Promise<() => void> {\n    20\t    let release: () => void = () => undefined;\n    21\t    const mine = new Promise<void>((resolve) => {\n    22\t      release = resolve;\n    23\t    });\n    24\t    const previous = this.#tail;\n    25\t    this.#tail = previous.then(() => mine);\n    26\t    await previous;\n    27\t    const timer = setTimeout(release, this.#maxHoldMs);\n    28\t    timer.unref();\n    29\t    return () => {\n    30\t      clearTimeout(timer);\n    31\t      release();\n    32\t    };\n    33\t  }\n    34\t}\n    35\t\n    36\texport interface GatedStart {\n    37\t  /** For `ConnectionOptions.command`: waits for the gate, then yields the bridge argv. */\n    38\t  command: () => Promise<readonly string[]>;\n    39\t  /** For `ConnectionOptions.onLog`: frees the gate when the bridge reports ready. */\n    40\t  onLog: (text: string) => void;\n    41\t  /** Frees the gate if this connection still holds it (on close). */\n    42\t  release: () => void;\n    43\t}\n    44\t\n    45\t/** Wires one connection's bridge launches through `gate`; every respawn takes a new turn. */\n    46\texport function gatedStart(gate: StartGate, argv: readonly string[]): GatedStart {\n    47\t  let release: (() => void) | undefined;\n    48\t  const free = (): void => {\n    49\t    release?.();\n    50\t    release = undefined;\n    51\t  };\n    52\t  return {\n    53\t    command: async () => {\n    54\t      free();\n    55\t      release = await gate.acquire();\n    56\t      return argv;\n    57\t    },\n    58\t    onLog: (text) => {\n    59\t      if (text.includes(BRIDGE_READY)) free();\n    60\t    },\n    61\t    release: free,\n    62\t  };\n","isError":false}]}
ENTRY 9594 {"id":9594,"kind":"pi.assistant","messages":[{"role":"assistant","text":"[thinking]## Findings\n\n### 1. Remote output ignores stdout backpressure\n- Severity: high\n- Confidence: high\n- Location: packages/sandbox-env/src/bridge.ts:64\n- What happens: If Pi reads slowly while the remote process produces output, `stdout.write()` eventually returns `false`, but the bridge continues consuming remote output and queues it in memory. A sufficiently large response can exhaust the bridge’s memory and terminate it, losing the response and disrupting the connection.\n- Evidence: `onStdout: (chunk) => void stdout.write(chunk)`; the SDK’s callback consumer continues pulling events without waiting for the writable to drain.\n- Fix idea: Consume remote events through a pull-based interface and pause consumption until stdout drains. Apply equivalent bounded handling to stderr.\n\n### 2. Closing a connection waiting for the start gate does not cancel its turn\n- Severity: medium\n- Confidence: high\n- Location: packages/sandbox-env/src/start-gate.ts:53\n- What happens: If a connection closes while its `command()` is waiting behind another startup, `release()` has no acquired release function to call. The queued acquisition remains; when its turn arrives, it holds the gate until readiness or the 45-second timeout, delaying live connections behind it. Multiple closed waiters can compound the delay.\n- Evidence: `release = await gate.acquire()` assigns `release` only after waiting, while `free()` only calls `release?.()`.\n- Fix idea: Make queued acquisitions cancellable, or record that the gated start was closed and immediately release any subsequently acquired turn without launching.\n\n### 3. A stdout failure during the final flush can be reported as success\n- Severity: medium\n- Confidence: high\n- Location: packages/sandbox-env/src/bridge.ts:49\n- What happens: If the remote process exits successfully but a buffered stdout write fails during the final flush, the flush callback resolves regardless of its error. The bridge can then return the remote exit code, including zero, even though the parent did not receive the complete output.\n- Evidence: `stream.write(new Uint8Array(0), () => resolve())`; after the exit wins the race, `await flush(stdout)` is not raced against `outputFailed`.\n- Fix idea: Reject the flush on write errors and treat that rejection as a fatal bridge failure, including errors emitted while the flush is pending.\n\n## Areas checked with no findings\n- Token derivation and bridge environment allowlisting for master-key and cross-sandbox token exposure.\n- Bridge argument parsing and target selection.\n- Sequential stdin writes and remote stdin closure.\n- Sandbox handle cleanup on normal process closure and start failure.\n\nEND-OF-REPORT"}]}
